Here’s how this can be structured for a strong, to-the-point response that impresses interviewers:

1. Enable Authentication
Make sure only authorized users can access Jenkins. Use its user database or connect it to Google, etc.

2. Configure Authorization Carefully
Don’t give everyone full access. Use role-based or matrix-based authorization to define what each user can access or modify. 

3. Enforce HTTPS
 Jenkins should never be exposed over plain HTTP. Secure it via HTTPS using reverse proxies like Nginx or Apache, or configure SSL directly.

4. Keep Jenkins and Plugins Updated
Outdated plugins or Jenkins versions can be risky. Keep them updated regularly.

5. Use the Credentials Plugin Properly
Use Jenkins Credentials Manager to store passwords, API keys, etc. Never put them in scripts or job configs.

6. Restrict Script & CLI Access
Only admins should be able to run scripts or use the Jenkins CLI. Disable it if not needed.

7. Monitor and Audit Activities
Track what’s happening using audit plugins or connect Jenkins to logging tools.

8. Secure Agent Connections
Make sure only trusted agents can connect. Use firewalls and secure connections.

9. Regular Backups
 Back up JENKINS_HOME, job configurations, and credentials frequently. Automate the process and test restores periodically.
Backup JENKINS_HOME and configurations and credentials frequently so nothing is lost in case of failure.

Tip:
In interviews, don’t just name these points. Explain why each one matters. That shows you actually understand Jenkins security.
